1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,600
Welcome to Topic 7.

2
00:00:01,850 --> 00:00:03,450
2, Creating Virtual Machines.

3
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:04,900
In Topic 7.

4
00:00:05,150 --> 00:00:08,350
1 you built the host and its switches.

5
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:10,600
Now you add virtual machines.

6
00:00:10,850 --> 00:00:16,050
You will walk through the New Virtual
Machine Wizard and choose a generation.

7
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:21,100
You will set memory, including Dynamic
Memory, and the number of virtual

8
00:00:21,350 --> 00:00:22,550
processors.

9
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:28,800
You will compare disk formats and disk
types, and build a VM on a differencing

10
00:00:29,050 --> 00:00:30,250
disk.

11
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:35,300
Finally you will look at integration
services and Enhanced Session Mode, and

12
00:00:35,550 --> 00:00:37,950
see the PowerShell for every task.

13
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:43,000
In Hyper-V Manager, select New in the
Action pane, then Virtual Machine.

14
00:00:43,250 --> 00:00:50,050
The left pane lists every page in order,
from the name of the VM to the summary.

15
00:00:50,300 --> 00:00:53,100
This slide shows the Specify Generation
page.

16
00:00:53,350 --> 00:00:57,750
Choose Generation 2, which gives you UEFI
firmware and Secure Boot.

17
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:03,600
Microsoft's advice is to create Generation
2 unless you have a specific reason not

18
00:01:03,850 --> 00:01:05,050
to.

19
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:11,300
Read the warning on the page: once the VM
exists, you cannot change its generation.

20
00:01:11,550 --> 00:01:14,350
Any page you skip keeps its default.

21
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:16,200
So what really differs?

22
00:01:16,450 --> 00:01:20,050
Generation 1 uses old BIOS firmware and
emulated hardware.

23
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:27,500
It boots from an IDE disk, an ISO or a
physical DVD drive, a floppy, or a legacy

24
00:01:27,750 --> 00:01:28,950
network adapter.

25
00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:33,200
Generation 2 uses UEFI with Secure Boot on
by default.

26
00:01:33,450 --> 00:01:39,850
It boots from a SCSI disk in VHDX format,
an ISO file, or a standard network

27
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:41,300
adapter.

28
00:01:41,550 --> 00:01:45,150
It cannot use a physical DVD drive at all.

29
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:50,600
Remember, generation belongs to the VM,
not the disk, and it is final.

30
00:01:50,850 --> 00:01:53,250
Our lab VMs are Generation 2.

31
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:59,100
Without Dynamic Memory, a VM keeps all of
its startup memory all the time.

32
00:01:59,350 --> 00:02:04,150
With Dynamic Memory, Hyper-V moves memory
between VMs as they need it.

33
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:07,600
VM1 starts with 2048 megabytes, its
startup RAM.

34
00:02:07,850 --> 00:02:13,850
When it sits idle, Hyper-V can take memory
back, but never below the minimum, 512

35
00:02:14,100 --> 00:02:15,300
megabytes.

36
00:02:15,550 --> 00:02:20,350
When it gets busy, Hyper-V adds memory
again, never above the maximum.

37
00:02:20,600 --> 00:02:24,600
The buffer is spare memory kept free
inside the VM.

38
00:02:24,850 --> 00:02:28,850
Memory weight decides who wins when the
host runs short.

39
00:02:29,100 --> 00:02:35,100
Smart Paging uses disk as memory only
while a VM restarts on a full host.

40
00:02:35,350 --> 00:02:37,750
Here is where you set it.

41
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:42,000
Right-click the VM, choose Settings, and
select Memory under Hardware.

42
00:02:42,250 --> 00:02:46,250
Startup RAM is the memory the VM gets at
boot.

43
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:48,900
Below it, select Enable Dynamic Memory.

44
00:02:49,150 --> 00:02:54,350
You can only turn that on or off while the
VM is off.

45
00:02:54,600 --> 00:02:57,800
Then type the Minimum RAM and Maximum RAM.

46
00:02:58,050 --> 00:03:00,450
Startup must sit between the two.

47
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:06,300
Memory buffer is a percentage, and the
Memory weight slider runs from Low to

48
00:03:06,550 --> 00:03:07,750
High.

49
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:13,600
While the VM runs, you can still raise the
maximum or lower the minimum.

50
00:03:13,850 --> 00:03:15,050
Next, processors.

51
00:03:15,300 --> 00:03:21,300
Each virtual processor runs on one of the
host's logical processors when it has work

52
00:03:21,550 --> 00:03:22,750
to do.

53
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:24,200
VM1 gets two.

54
00:03:24,450 --> 00:03:27,650
Change the count while the VM is off.

55
00:03:27,900 --> 00:03:30,300
NUMA stands for Non-Uniform Memory Access.

56
00:03:30,550 --> 00:03:37,350
Each CPU socket has memory close to it,
and a VM that fits inside one NUMA node

57
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:38,800
runs fastest.

58
00:03:39,050 --> 00:03:44,650
The Processor page also has resource
control: a reserve, a limit and a relative

59
00:03:44,900 --> 00:03:46,100
weight.

60
00:03:46,350 --> 00:03:49,550
Now the Server Plus view: plan the host.

61
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:51,400
HV01 has 8 gigabytes.

62
00:03:51,650 --> 00:03:56,450
Processors can be shared by many VMs, but
memory cannot be overcommitted.

63
00:03:56,700 --> 00:04:00,700
A virtual hard disk is a file on the host.

64
00:04:00,950 --> 00:04:02,550
There are two formats.

65
00:04:02,800 --> 00:04:04,400
VHDX is the default.

66
00:04:04,650 --> 00:04:11,050
It can be up to 64 terabytes and protects
its own metadata if the power fails.

67
00:04:11,300 --> 00:04:17,700
VHD is the older format, up to about 2
terabytes, still used for old hosts and

68
00:04:17,950 --> 00:04:19,550
for uploads to Azure.

69
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:21,800
Then there are three types.

70
00:04:22,050 --> 00:04:29,250
A fixed size disk takes all its space on
day one; it is fast and has no surprises.

71
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:32,700
A dynamically expanding disk starts small
and grows.

72
00:04:32,950 --> 00:04:37,350
A differencing disk stores only the
changes to a parent disk.

73
00:04:37,600 --> 00:04:41,600
Let's look closer at differencing disks,
because VM1 uses one.

74
00:04:41,850 --> 00:04:44,650
The parent is the prepared base disk.

75
00:04:44,900 --> 00:04:48,900
VM1's own file, VM1 dot vhdx, holds only
VM1's changes.

76
00:04:49,150 --> 00:04:55,150
When VM1 reads a block it has not changed,
the read goes to the parent.

77
00:04:55,400 --> 00:04:57,800
Every write goes to the child.

78
00:04:58,050 --> 00:05:04,050
Many children can share one parent, and a
child can even be a parent itself.

79
00:05:04,300 --> 00:05:07,100
The big rule: never change the parent.

80
00:05:07,350 --> 00:05:09,750
If it changes, every child breaks.

81
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:12,400
So make the parent read-only first.

82
00:05:12,650 --> 00:05:16,650
In Hyper-V Manager, select Edit Disk in
the Action pane.

83
00:05:16,900 --> 00:05:22,500
The wizard asks you to locate the disk,
choose an action, configure it, and

84
00:05:22,750 --> 00:05:23,950
finish.

85
00:05:24,200 --> 00:05:29,000
Compact makes a dynamic or differencing
file smaller by removing empty blocks.

86
00:05:29,250 --> 00:05:36,050
Convert copies the disk to a new file, for
example VHD to VHDX, or dynamic to fixed.

87
00:05:36,300 --> 00:05:41,900
Expand makes the disk bigger; after that
you extend the volume inside the guest.

88
00:05:42,150 --> 00:05:48,550
Other actions, such as Shrink and Merge,
show up only when they fit the disk you

89
00:05:48,800 --> 00:05:50,000
picked.

90
00:05:50,250 --> 00:05:52,250
Here is VM1 in PowerShell.

91
00:05:52,500 --> 00:05:58,100
First, a variable holds the path of the
base disk, and Set-ItemProperty makes it

92
00:05:58,350 --> 00:05:59,550
read-only.

93
00:05:59,800 --> 00:06:05,400
New-VHD with the Differencing switch and
the ParentPath creates VM1 dot vhdx as a

94
00:06:05,650 --> 00:06:06,850
child.

95
00:06:07,100 --> 00:06:12,700
New-VM creates the VM with Generation 2,
2048 megabytes, the new disk and the

96
00:06:12,950 --> 00:06:14,150
Internal switch.

97
00:06:14,400 --> 00:06:19,200
Be careful: if you leave out Generation 2,
you get Generation 1.

98
00:06:19,450 --> 00:06:25,450
Set-VMMemory turns on Dynamic Memory with
a minimum of 512 and a maximum of 2048.

99
00:06:25,700 --> 00:06:27,300
Set-VMProcessor sets two processors.

100
00:06:27,550 --> 00:06:28,750
VM2 is different.

101
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:34,200
It gets a brand new disk, so New-VM uses
NewVHDPath and NewVHDSizeBytes, 60

102
00:06:34,450 --> 00:06:35,650
gigabytes.

103
00:06:35,900 --> 00:06:38,300
New-VM always makes that disk dynamic.

104
00:06:38,550 --> 00:06:44,950
A Generation 2 VM made by New-VM has no
DVD drive, so Add-VMDvdDrive adds one with

105
00:06:45,200 --> 00:06:46,800
the Windows Server ISO.

106
00:06:47,050 --> 00:06:53,050
Set-VMFirmware puts the DVD drive first in
the boot order, so Windows Setup can start

107
00:06:53,300 --> 00:06:54,500
from the ISO.

108
00:06:54,750 --> 00:06:59,950
VM1's disk is differencing, and its size,
127 gigabytes, comes from the parent.

109
00:07:00,200 --> 00:07:03,800
VM2's disk is dynamic, 60 gigabytes, with
no parent.

110
00:07:04,050 --> 00:07:09,250
Integration services are small services
inside the guest that talk to the host

111
00:07:09,500 --> 00:07:12,300
over the VMBus, a fast internal channel.

112
00:07:12,550 --> 00:07:15,350
In Settings, under Management, select
Integration Services.

113
00:07:15,600 --> 00:07:20,000
Operating system shutdown lets the host
shut the guest down cleanly.

114
00:07:20,250 --> 00:07:24,650
Time synchronization keeps the guest clock
in step with the host.

115
00:07:24,900 --> 00:07:26,900
Data Exchange shares simple facts.

116
00:07:27,150 --> 00:07:30,350
Heartbeat tells the host the guest is
alive.

117
00:07:30,600 --> 00:07:33,400
Backup lets the host take consistent
backups.

118
00:07:33,650 --> 00:07:37,650
Guest services is the one that is off by
default.

119
00:07:37,900 --> 00:07:43,100
You need it to copy files in from the
host, in Lab 7.

120
00:07:43,350 --> 00:07:44,550
3.

121
00:07:44,800 --> 00:07:48,800
Virtual Machine Connection, or VMConnect,
normally shows a basic console.

122
00:07:49,050 --> 00:07:54,650
Enhanced Session Mode uses Remote Desktop
over the VMBus, so you get the clipboard,

123
00:07:54,900 --> 00:07:57,300
local drives and a resizable window.

124
00:07:57,550 --> 00:07:59,150
It takes two sides.

125
00:07:59,400 --> 00:08:04,600
On the host, two settings: the server
policy Allow enhanced session mode, which

126
00:08:04,850 --> 00:08:10,450
is off by default on Windows Server, and
the user setting Use enhanced session

127
00:08:10,700 --> 00:08:11,900
mode.

128
00:08:12,150 --> 00:08:13,350
Lab 7.

129
00:08:13,600 --> 00:08:14,800
1 set both.

130
00:08:15,050 --> 00:08:20,650
In the guest, Microsoft Learn says Remote
Desktop Services must be enabled, and the

131
00:08:20,900 --> 00:08:25,300
VM must run at least Windows 10 or Windows
Server 2016.

132
00:08:25,550 --> 00:08:31,550
Learn does not mention Server Core guests,
so in the lab you record what VMConnect

133
00:08:31,800 --> 00:08:33,000
shows.

134
00:08:33,250 --> 00:08:35,250
Two more pages in Settings.

135
00:08:35,500 --> 00:08:41,100
On the Security page, Enable Secure Boot
is selected, and the template is Microsoft

136
00:08:41,350 --> 00:08:42,550
Windows.

137
00:08:42,800 --> 00:08:46,000
Secure Boot lets only signed boot loaders
start.

138
00:08:46,250 --> 00:08:51,050
For a Linux guest you would pick the
Microsoft UEFI Certificate Authority

139
00:08:51,300 --> 00:08:52,500
template.

140
00:08:52,750 --> 00:08:58,750
Automatic Stop Action decides what happens
to the VM when the host shuts down: save

141
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:05,000
its state, which is the default, turn it
off, or shut down the guest cleanly.

142
00:09:05,250 --> 00:09:08,050
In the lab you choose shut down.

143
00:09:08,300 --> 00:09:13,100
Automatic Start Action and the Smart
Paging file folder are set nearby.

144
00:09:13,350 --> 00:09:15,350
The same settings in PowerShell.

145
00:09:15,600 --> 00:09:17,200
Get-VMIntegrationService lists the
services.

146
00:09:17,450 --> 00:09:22,650
The PowerShell names differ a little from
the Settings window; for example Data

147
00:09:22,900 --> 00:09:27,700
Exchange is Key-Value Pair Exchange, and
Guest services is Guest Service Interface,

148
00:09:27,950 --> 00:09:29,150
which shows False.

149
00:09:29,400 --> 00:09:33,400
Enable-VMIntegrationService turns it on,
even while the VM is running.

150
00:09:33,650 --> 00:09:38,050
Set-VM sets the automatic start and stop
actions in one line.

151
00:09:38,300 --> 00:09:42,300
Get-VMFirmware shows Secure Boot on with
the Microsoft Windows template.

152
00:09:42,550 --> 00:09:47,350
And Get-VMHost shows the Enhanced Session
Mode policy, and Set-VMHost would change

153
00:09:47,600 --> 00:09:48,800
it.

154
00:09:49,050 --> 00:09:50,650
Four mistakes to avoid.

155
00:09:50,900 --> 00:09:53,300
One: running New-VM without Generation 2.

156
00:09:53,550 --> 00:10:00,350
You get Generation 1, and you cannot fix
it; you delete the VM and create it again.

157
00:10:00,600 --> 00:10:04,600
Two: booting or editing the parent of a
differencing disk.

158
00:10:04,850 --> 00:10:08,050
Every child breaks, so make the parent
read-only.

159
00:10:08,300 --> 00:10:11,900
Three: many dynamic disks on a small host
volume.

160
00:10:12,150 --> 00:10:16,150
They grow until the volume fills and the
VMs pause.

161
00:10:16,400 --> 00:10:17,600
Watch free space.

162
00:10:17,850 --> 00:10:23,050
Four: missing the prompt Press any key to
boot from CD or DVD.

163
00:10:23,300 --> 00:10:26,100
Open VMConnect first, then start the VM.

164
00:10:26,350 --> 00:10:31,550
Remember VM1: Generation 2, Dynamic Memory
from 512 to 2048 megabytes, and a

165
00:10:31,800 --> 00:10:34,200
differencing disk on the base image.

166
00:10:34,450 --> 00:10:38,450
First, a VM must boot from a physical DVD
drive.

167
00:10:38,700 --> 00:10:40,700
Which generation do you choose?

168
00:10:40,950 --> 00:10:44,950
Second, VM1 sits idle while the other VMs
are busy.

169
00:10:45,200 --> 00:10:48,000
What range of memory can it have?

170
00:10:48,250 --> 00:10:52,650
Third, a classmate boots a VM straight
from the base disk.

171
00:10:52,900 --> 00:10:54,500
What happens to VM1?

172
00:10:54,750 --> 00:10:56,750
Fourth, Copy-VMFile to VM1 fails.

173
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,000
Which integration service is missing?

174
00:10:59,250 --> 00:11:02,050
Pause the video and answer all four.

175
00:11:02,300 --> 00:11:03,500
The answers.

176
00:11:03,750 --> 00:11:04,950
One: Generation 1.

177
00:11:05,200 --> 00:11:11,600
Generation 2 VMs accept only ISO files in
their DVD drive, and you can't change the

178
00:11:11,850 --> 00:11:13,050
generation later.

179
00:11:13,300 --> 00:11:16,100
Two: anywhere between 512 and 2048
megabytes.

180
00:11:16,350 --> 00:11:21,950
Hyper-V can take memory back down to the
minimum, and memory weight decides who

181
00:11:22,200 --> 00:11:24,600
wins when the host is short.

182
00:11:24,850 --> 00:11:30,450
Three: VM1's disk breaks, because the
parent changed under it and the blocks no

183
00:11:30,700 --> 00:11:31,900
longer match.

184
00:11:32,150 --> 00:11:35,350
That is exactly why the parent is
read-only.

185
00:11:35,600 --> 00:11:40,400
Four: Guest services, the one integration
service that is off by default.

186
00:11:40,650 --> 00:11:43,050
Let's see it in the pod.

187
00:11:43,300 --> 00:11:48,900
On HV01 I make the base disk read-only and
create VM1's differencing disk with

188
00:11:49,150 --> 00:11:50,350
New-VHD.

189
00:11:50,600 --> 00:11:56,200
In Hyper-V Manager I run the wizard: VM1,
Generation 2, 2048 megabytes, the Internal

190
00:11:56,450 --> 00:11:58,450
switch, and the existing disk.

191
00:11:58,700 --> 00:12:04,300
In Settings I set the memory range, two
processors, and turn on Guest services.

192
00:12:04,550 --> 00:12:07,350
I start VM1 and set the password.

193
00:12:07,600 --> 00:12:14,800
Then I build VM2 with a new 60 gigabyte
disk and the ISO, press a key at the

194
00:12:15,050 --> 00:12:18,250
prompt, and stop at the first Setup
screen.

195
00:12:18,500 --> 00:12:20,100
Get-VHD shows both disks.

196
00:12:20,350 --> 00:12:21,550
To wrap up.

197
00:12:21,800 --> 00:12:27,000
Choose Generation 2 unless you need old
hardware, and remember the choice is

198
00:12:27,250 --> 00:12:28,450
final.

199
00:12:28,700 --> 00:12:33,500
Dynamic Memory moves memory between the
minimum and the maximum you set.

200
00:12:33,750 --> 00:12:38,950
Use VHDX; pick fixed for speed, dynamic
for space, and differencing for many

201
00:12:39,200 --> 00:12:42,400
copies of one image, with a read-only
parent.

202
00:12:42,650 --> 00:12:48,250
Guest services is off by default, and
Enhanced Session Mode needs both the host

203
00:12:48,500 --> 00:12:49,700
and the guest.

204
00:12:49,950 --> 00:12:51,150
In Lab 7.

205
00:12:51,400 --> 00:12:55,800
2, about 90 minutes and practice only, you
rebuild Lab 7.

206
00:12:56,050 --> 00:12:59,650
1, create VM1 and VM2, and compare their
disks.
